{"rows":[{"n":1,"file":"topics/01-java-core-jvm.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":2,"file":"topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":3,"file":"topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":4,"file":"topics/04-rest-grpc-webflux.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":5,"file":"topics/05-db-core-index-lock.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":6,"file":"topics/06-db-scaling.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":7,"file":"topics/07-sql-nosql-db-engines.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":8,"file":"topics/08-message-queue.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":9,"file":"topics/09-distributed-tx-fintech.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":12,"file":"topics/12-architecture-patterns.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":13,"file":"topics/13-security-oauth2.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":14,"file":"topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":15,"file":"topics/15-network-i-o-models.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":16,"file":"topics/16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.json","topic_type":"algorithm","system_design_items":["16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q1","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q2","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q3","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q4","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q5","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.whiteboard-dsa.q1"]},{"n":17,"file":"topics/17-rest-api-design.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":18,"file":"topics/18-query-optimization.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":19,"file":"topics/19-dsa-leetcode.json","topic_type":"algorithm"},{"n":20,"file":"topics/20-observability-sre.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":21,"file":"topics/21-linux-production-debug.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":22,"file":"topics/22-low-level-design-ood.json","topic_type":"algorithm"},{"n":23,"file":"topics/23-java-concurrency-coding.json","topic_type":"algorithm"},{"n":24,"file":"topics/24-domain-driven-design.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":25,"topic_type":"microservice","file":"topics/25-microservice.json"},{"n":26,"file":"topics/26-testing-strategy.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":28,"file":"topics/28-distributed-lock-lease.json","topic_type":"design"}],"row":{"n":2,"file":"topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.json","topic_type":"core"},"meta":{"topic_type":"core","key":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go","vi":{"label":"Java 8→25 & Java vs Go","title":"Tiến hoá Java 8 → 25 (LTS) & so kè với Go","intro":"Một senior cần kể được câu chuyện ngôn ngữ đã đi đâu trong 10 năm, và định vị Java so với Go ở thời điểm 2025–2026 khi cả hai hội tụ về concurrency nhẹ. Phần này được cập nhật theo dữ liệu mới nhất.","tags":["LTS cadence","Records/Sealed","Pattern matching","Virtual threads","Java 25","Java vs Go","GraalVM"]},"en":{"label":"Java 8→25 & Java vs Go","title":"Java 8 → 25 (LTS) evolution & Java vs Go","intro":"A senior should be able to tell the story of where the language went over ten years, and place Java against Go as of 2025–2026, now that both have converged on lightweight concurrency.","tags":["LTS cadence","Records/Sealed","Pattern matching","Virtual threads","Java 25","Java vs Go","GraalVM"]},"created_at":"2026-07-31","updated_at":"2026-10-25"},"topicMeta":{"1":{"en":{"label":"Java Core & JVM"},"vi":{"label":"Java Core & JVM"}},"2":{"en":{"label":"Java 8→25 & Java vs Go"},"vi":{"label":"Java 8→25 & Java vs Go"}},"3":{"en":{"label":"Spring Boot deep dive + build"},"vi":{"label":"Spring Boot deep + build"}},"4":{"en":{"label":"REST/gRPC & WebFlux"},"vi":{"label":"REST/gRPC & WebFlux"}},"5":{"en":{"label":"DB core: indexes & locking"},"vi":{"label":"DB core: index & lock"}},"6":{"en":{"label":"DB scaling"},"vi":{"label":"DB scaling"}},"7":{"en":{"label":"SQL/NoSQL & DB engines"},"vi":{"label":"SQL/NoSQL & DB engines"}},"8":{"en":{"label":"Message queue"},"vi":{"label":"Message queue"}},"9":{"en":{"label":"Distributed tx (fintech)"},"vi":{"label":"Distributed tx (fintech)"}},"10":{"en":{"label":"System design + rate limit"},"vi":{"label":"System design + rate limit"}},"11":{"en":{"label":"System design cases"},"vi":{"label":"System design cases"}},"12":{"en":{"label":"Architecture & patterns"},"vi":{"label":"Architecture & patterns"}},"13":{"en":{"label":"Security & OAuth2"},"vi":{"label":"Security & OAuth2"}},"14":{"en":{"label":"DevOps · K8s · best practices"},"vi":{"label":"DevOps · K8s · best practices"}},"15":{"en":{"label":"Network & I/O models"},"vi":{"label":"Network & I/O models"}},"16":{"en":{"label":"Project · concurrency · whiteboard"},"vi":{"label":"Project · concurrency · whiteboard"}},"17":{"en":{"label":"REST API design"},"vi":{"label":"Thiết kế REST API"}},"18":{"en":{"label":"Query optimisation"},"vi":{"label":"Tối ưu query"}},"19":{"en":{"label":"DSA & LeetCode"},"vi":{"label":"DSA & LeetCode"}},"20":{"en":{"label":"Observability & SRE"},"vi":{"label":"Observability & SRE"}},"21":{"en":{"label":"Linux & production debug"},"vi":{"label":"Linux & production debug"}},"22":{"en":{"label":"Low-level design / OOD"},"vi":{"label":"Low-level design / OOD"}},"23":{"en":{"label":"Java concurrency coding"},"vi":{"label":"Java concurrency coding"}},"24":{"en":{"label":"Domain-Driven Design"},"vi":{"label":"Domain-Driven Design"}},"25":{"en":{"label":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track"},"vi":{"label":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track"}},"26":{"en":{"label":"Testing"},"vi":{"label":"Testing"}},"27":{"en":{"label":"API Gateway & Identity Edge"},"vi":{"label":"API Gateway & Identity Edge"}},"28":{"en":{"label":"Distributed Lock & Lease"},"vi":{"label":"Distributed Lock & Lease"}}},"reviewedAt":{"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q5":"2026-08-01","02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q6":"2026-10-25","02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q7":"2026-08-01","02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q9":"2026-10-25"},"lang":"en","body":{"sections":[{"title":"Release timeline","items":[{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q1","q":"How did Java 8 change the way Java is written?","a":"Java 8's turning point was a set of final Java SE language and library features that changed everyday code:\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Status</th><th>Practical boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Lambda expressions, default methods, streams</td><td>final</td><td>Java SE 8; choose the pipeline or a loop by readability and workload</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Optional</td><td>final</td><td>Java SE 8; makes absence explicit but does not prevent every null bug</td></tr>\n<tr><td>java.time</td><td>final</td><td>Java SE 8; use the immutable, timezone-aware types for new code</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nMany estates still run Java 8, but it is not a universal enterprise default. Whether to stay depends on the selected JDK distribution's support and security policy, framework/dependency compatibility, and migration budget.\n\n:::deep\n- Streams are [[b:lazy]] until a terminal operation; <code>parallelStream()</code> uses the common ForkJoinPool, so treat it carefully for I/O or web-container work.\n- <code>Optional</code> is useful at an API boundary, especially as a return type for an absent value. It is not a blanket replacement for validation, fields, or parameters.\n- <code>java.time</code> gives immutable types such as <code>Instant</code> and <code>ZonedDateTime</code>; keep local business time and UTC instants distinct.\n- Default methods can evolve an interface, but conflicting defaults and behavioral compatibility still need review.\n- Lambda translation uses <code>invokedynamic</code> and <code>LambdaMetafactory</code> as an implementation detail. Do not turn that detail into a universal class-count or performance promise.\n\n[[r:Pitfall]]: nested streams can be harder to read and debug than a plain loop. Keep the abstraction that makes the invariant easiest to review.\n:::","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q2","q":"What does Java 11 bring that matters (compared to 8)?","a":"Java 11 is best understood as a release with both final features and migration boundaries:\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Feature or change</th><th>Status</th><th>Migration meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>var</code> for local variables</td><td>final</td><td>Final in JDK 10 and available on Java 11; it is compile-time inference, not dynamic typing</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Standard <code>HttpClient</code></td><td>final</td><td>Java 11 API with HTTP/2 and asynchronous operations; test timeout and cancellation behavior</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Single-file source launch</td><td>final</td><td>JDK 11 tool behavior; it does not replace a production build/reproducibility policy</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Java EE and CORBA modules</td><td>removed from the JDK</td><td>Add and pin required dependencies; test JAXB, JAX-WS, CORBA, reflection, and packaging explicitly</td></tr>\n<tr><td>G1 default, ZGC, and Epsilon</td><td>implementation/vendor-specific</td><td>Verify the selected distribution, collector availability, flags, and container ergonomics</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nDo not use the release number to predict migration pain. An 8→11 hop can expose removed modules and stronger encapsulation, while a later hop can expose agents, bytecode tools, drivers, TLS defaults, or container assumptions.\n\n:::deep\nTreat every hop as a test matrix:\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Gate</th><th>Evidence before rollout</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Removed modules and APIs</td><td>dependency scan, compile, startup, and representative integration tests</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Strong encapsulation, agents, and reflection</td><td>agent attach/startup tests plus reflective and proxy-heavy paths</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Serialization, JDBC, and TLS</td><td>wire/schema compatibility, driver matrix, certificate, and protocol tests</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Runtime and container limits</td><td>GC/thread/heap behavior under the production cgroup and failure budget</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Preview or incubator flags</td><td>compile and run with the exact pinned flags, or remove the feature from the target</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nUse 02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q7 for the support-policy decision. The migration conclusion is evidence from this matrix, not “8→11 is always the hardest” or “11→17→21 is only dependency bumping.”\n:::","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q3","q":"How did Java 13–16 lay the groundwork for 17 (text blocks, switch, records)?","a":"Java 13–16 were transition releases: several features were previewed, then finalized on a later release.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Status timeline</th><th>What to carry forward</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Switch expressions</td><td>preview in JDK 12–13; final in JDK 14</td><td>value-producing switch and arrow cases; verify syntax against the target JDK</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Text blocks</td><td>preview in JDK 13–14; final in JDK 15</td><td>multi-line SQL/JSON/HTML is clearer, but escaping and formatting remain application concerns</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Records</td><td>preview in JDK 14–15; final in JDK 16</td><td>record components are final references, not recursively immutable objects</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sealed classes</td><td>preview in JDK 15–16; final in JDK 17</td><td>the permitted hierarchy can support exhaustive analysis once the compiler knows its variants</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pattern matching for <code>instanceof</code></td><td>preview in JDK 14–15; final in JDK 16</td><td>less manual casting; null behavior and flow scoping still matter</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Helpful NullPointerException</td><td>final in JDK 14</td><td>diagnostic detail is useful evidence, not a substitute for fixing the invariant</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n:::deep\nA preview feature is not a stable production contract. <code>--enable-preview</code> must match the compiler and runtime, and the source can change before finalization. A final feature still needs a target-runtime and library compatibility test.\n\nThis is why Java 17 contains several final forms of work that began earlier; “introduced in 17” and “finalized in 17” are different statements. Keep the JEP status beside the feature when writing a migration plan.\n:::","difficulty":"extra"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q4","q":"Java 17 (LTS) — what's the key feature set?","a":"Java 17 provides a strong final baseline, but its guarantees have precise boundaries:\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Feature or behavior</th><th>Status in Java 17</th><th>Boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Records</td><td>final</td><td>A shallowly immutable carrier: component references are final, while referenced objects may still mutate</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sealed classes</td><td>final</td><td>Restricts permitted direct subclasses; it does not define serialization, validation, or business-state rules</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pattern matching for <code>instanceof</code></td><td>final</td><td>Reduces casts; flow scope, null, and type tests remain part of the contract</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pattern matching for <code>switch</code> and record patterns</td><td>preview or not yet available in the Java 17 target</td><td>Use the status of the exact target JDK; both were final in Java 21</td></tr>\n<tr><td>JDK-internal encapsulation</td><td>platform/runtime behavior</td><td>Older reflection and agent assumptions can fail; test the actual libraries and flags</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nJava 17 is a vendor-supported LTS choice for many distributions, but support dates, patch channels, and framework compatibility are distribution- and date-specific. Pin those inputs instead of calling one release the universal recommendation.\n\n:::deep\n<code>sealed</code> + <code>record</code> + pattern matching is an [[g:ADT-like]] modeling style, not a Java feature formally named “algebraic data type”:\n\n- the compiler can reason about a known sealed hierarchy;\n- a record supplies a concise carrier, but deep immutability requires defensive copies or immutable components;\n- exhaustiveness does not settle <code>null</code>, serialization, persistence, or compatibility when a hierarchy evolves;\n- state-machine variants such as <code>Held | Paid | Issued | Cancelled</code> still need explicit transition and validation rules.\n\nJava 17's encapsulation changes and the Java 17 baseline for Spring Boot 3 are migration facts to verify alongside the dependency and agent matrix.\n:::","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q5","q":"Java 21 (LTS) — why is it a 'game-changer' for concurrency?","a":"Java 21 made several concurrency and data-model features final; other names in the same discussion were still preview or version-specific.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Status in or around Java 21</th><th>Boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Virtual Threads (JEP 444)</td><td>final</td><td>Useful for blocking-style concurrency; CPU work and downstream capacity still need bounds</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pattern matching for <code>switch</code> (JEP 441)</td><td>final</td><td>Guarded patterns do not cover values that fail the guard</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Record patterns (JEP 440)</td><td>final</td><td>Deconstruction follows the record's declared components and their null/type behavior</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sequenced Collections (JEP 431)</td><td>final</td><td>Standardizes first/last/reversed operations; it does not change collection capacity</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Generational ZGC</td><td>implementation/vendor-specific</td><td>Check the selected build, collector flags, and measured pause/CPU trade-off</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Structured Concurrency</td><td>preview</td><td>Preview in Java 21 and still preview in JDK 25; isolate flags and source usage</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Scoped Values</td><td>preview in Java 21</td><td>Final in JDK 25; do not back-port the later status to a Java 21 target</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Foreign Function &amp; Memory API</td><td>not final in Java 21</td><td>Final in JDK 22, so keep that milestone separate</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nAn exhaustive Java 21 <code>switch</code> must include an unguarded fallback for a variant whose guard is false:\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"k\">sealed interface</span> Event <span class=\"k\">permits</span> Refund, Charge {}\n<span class=\"k\">record</span> Refund(String id, <span class=\"k\">long</span> amountCents) <span class=\"k\">implements</span> Event {}\n<span class=\"k\">record</span> Charge(String id, <span class=\"k\">long</span> amountCents) <span class=\"k\">implements</span> Event {}\n<span class=\"c\">    </span>\n<span class=\"k\">switch</span> (event) {\n    <span class=\"k\">case</span> Refund(<span class=\"k\">var</span> id, <span class=\"k\">var</span> amount) <span class=\"k\">when</span> amount &gt; <span class=\"n\">0</span> -&gt; <span class=\"f\">refund</span>(id, amount);\n    <span class=\"k\">case</span> Refund(<span class=\"k\">var</span> id, <span class=\"k\">var</span> amount)                 -&gt; <span class=\"k\">throw new</span> IllegalArgumentException(<span class=\"s\">\"amount\"</span>);\n    <span class=\"k\">case</span> Charge c                                      -&gt; <span class=\"f\">charge</span>(c);\n    <span class=\"k\">case null</span>                                          -&gt; <span class=\"k\">throw new</span> IllegalArgumentException(<span class=\"s\">\"event\"</span>);\n}</code></pre>\n\n:::deep\nThe migration value is larger than a feature checklist:\n\n- Virtual threads can keep blocking JDBC/HTTP code readable, but CPU-bound work needs bounded parallelism and the database, partner quota, queue, deadline, and memory budgets remain finite.\n- Monitor-related pinning changed in JDK 24; native calls and other blocking paths still require an audit on the target runtime.\n- A guarded pattern covers only values satisfying its guard, so the second <code>Refund</code> arm is required.\n- Scoped Values became final in JDK 25, while Structured Concurrency remains preview there. Keep those lifecycle statuses separate in build and rollout policy.\n\nFor a Spring Boot blocking service, enabling virtual threads can be a small configuration change; capacity tests decide whether it is safe.\n:::","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q6","q":"Java 25 (GA 9/2025) — what matters most for production and high-load systems?","a":"Java 25 should be read feature by feature. Treat its LTS position, support duration, patch channel, and cost as properties of the selected JDK distribution rather than universal Java guarantees.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Feature or change</th><th>Status</th><th>Production boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Scoped Values (JEP 506)</td><td>final</td><td>Immutable, bounded context propagation in JDK 25; test scope lifetime and inheritance</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Compact Object Headers (JEP 519)</td><td>final feature, build/flag-specific</td><td>Header layout and heap savings depend on compressed references, fields, alignment, and the selected build; measure them<br>Opt-in in 25; the default from JDK 27 (JEP 534)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Generational Shenandoah</td><td>product feature; implementation/vendor-specific</td><td>Availability and default differ by distribution; select and benchmark it against the target collectors</td></tr>\n<tr><td>AOT command-line ergonomics and method profiling</td><td>implementation/toolchain-specific</td><td>Profiles are optimization artifacts tied to a build and representative training workload</td></tr>\n<tr><td>JFR sampling and method tracing additions</td><td>implementation/version-specific</td><td>Verify the exact JDK build, event support, overhead, and operational export path</td></tr>\n<tr><td>KDF API, module imports, compact source files, and flexible constructor bodies</td><td>final features in JDK 25</td><td>Still run compiler, framework, and style/tooling compatibility tests</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Structured Concurrency (JEP 505)</td><td>preview</td><td>Requires the matching preview policy and can change before finalization<br>Still preview in JDK 27 (JEP 533), and its API changed in 26 and 27</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Foreign Function &amp; Memory API</td><td>final since JDK 22</td><td>It is not a new Java 25 finalization; native boundaries still need security and failure tests</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n:::tip Production positioning\nCanary product features and compare application SLOs, CPU, RSS, GC behavior, readiness, and rollback time. A feature marked final can still be a poor choice for a particular workload, while a preview feature needs an explicit compatibility and rollback policy.\n:::\n\n:::deep\nCompact Object Headers do not justify a universal “8-byte object” or heap-saving claim: the actual layout depends on the object and build. Use JOL or heap measurement on the target distribution.\n\nGenerational Shenandoah may suit a short-lived-object workload, but select it explicitly and benchmark it against G1/ZGC with the same traffic and cgroup limits.\n\nAOT method profiles come from a training run. If class paths, feature flags, or traffic differ from production, HotSpot still profiles and optimizes online; the cache is not a replacement for the JIT.\n\nStructured Concurrency uses <code>StructuredTaskScope.open(...)</code> in the JDK 25 preview and needs <code>--enable-preview</code>; Scoped Values are final. Keep those statuses distinct in documentation and CI.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q7","q":"Why do enterprises stick to LTS releases instead of the latest version every 6 months?","a":"“LTS” is a support commitment made by a JDK vendor, not a Java-language guarantee with one universal end date.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Policy input</th><th>What to record</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Support owner</td><td>vendor/distribution, architecture, support channel, security policy, and end date</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Update cadence</td><td>CPU/PSU rollout, canary, rollback, and emergency-patch procedure</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Compatibility scope</td><td>framework, agents, libraries, drivers, base image, and operating-system support</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Upgrade lead time</td><td>next-JDK CI signal, migration test budget, and production rollback rehearsal</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nEnterprises often choose a supported LTS to simplify certification, vulnerability patching, compliance evidence, and fleet testing.\n\nThat does [[r:not]] mean freezing the runtime: security and CPU/PSU updates still need a tested rollout path. Vendor designations and free/commercial windows differ, so verify them for the actual distribution.\n\n:::warn The migration risk is accumulated distance\nJumping from 8 directly to 21 or 25 combines removed APIs, strong encapsulation, library changes, TLS defaults, and build-tool upgrades. Regularly testing the next JDK keeps the dependency graph and operational playbook ready.\n:::\n\nThere is no universal “never fall more than two LTS releases behind” rule; derive the deadline from support, security exposure, framework support, and migration lead time.\n\n:::deep\nBefore adopting a target JDK, require evidence for each gate:\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Gate</th><th>Minimum evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Removed modules/APIs</td><td>dependency inventory, compile, startup, and integration tests</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Strong encapsulation, agents, and reflection</td><td>agent startup, proxy, reflective access, and serialization tests</td></tr>\n<tr><td>JDBC, TLS, and wire compatibility</td><td>driver matrix, certificate/protocol checks, and representative contract tests</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Container/runtime limits</td><td>CPU, heap, native memory, threads, GC, and shutdown tests under cgroup limits</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Preview/incubator use</td><td>exact compiler/runtime flags, isolated source boundary, and rollback plan</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nA non-LTS feature release can be reasonable when the team can upgrade frequently and needs a delivered feature. LTS is a maintenance strategy, not proof that an application is secure or stable.\n:::","difficulty":"extra"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q8","q":"How do you read Java release notes without accidentally shipping a preview or vendor-specific feature?","a":"**Classify every feature before using it in a production recommendation:**\n- a final Java SE API has a specification and compatibility contract\n- a preview feature requires the matching compiler/runtime preview flags and may change or disappear\n- an incubator module is an experimental API with a different adoption and migration risk\n- a HotSpot diagnostic flag or vendor default is not a Java language guarantee\n\nFor a pinned service, record the JDK distribution, exact patch version, compiler and runtime flags, base image, architecture, and whether preview modules are allowed.\n\nCompile and run with the same policy in CI, verify the feature with a small compatibility test, and make the release gate fail when the runtime is older or preview flags are missing. Use the official JEP/API/release note rather than a blog title as the status source.\n\nFor example, StructuredTaskScope is documented as a preview API in Java 25, while JEP 491 is a delivered Java 24 HotSpot change. Those facts have different rollout rules.\n\nA release table should therefore say `final`, `preview`, `incubator`, or `implementation-specific` next to each feature, then link the migration and rollback test. “Java 25 supports it” is incomplete unless the exact feature status and target distribution are named.","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q9","difficulty":"advanced","q":"Java 26 and 27 shipped after the Java 25 LTS. What changed that matters in production, and should a service on 25 move?","a":"Neither is an LTS release. JDK 26 reached GA on 17 March 2026 and JDK 27 on 15 September 2026. Oracle's roadmap plans the next LTS as Java 29 in September 2027, and each non-LTS release is superseded by the next feature release.\n\nA fleet standardised on 25 usually stays there and treats 26 and 27 as the early-warning build — what changes there is what the next LTS will change.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Change</th><th>Release · status</th><th>What it changes in production</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>G1 is the default collector in every environment (JEP 523)</td><td>27 · final</td><td>A JVM that saw one CPU or less than 1792 MB used to pick Serial<br>On 27 a small pod gets G1 unless a flag pins Serial</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Compact object headers by default (JEP 534)</td><td>27 · final</td><td>Headers shrink from 96 to 64 bits on 64-bit platforms (opt-in since JEP 519 in 25)<br><code>-XX:-UseCompactObjectHeaders</code> turns it off</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Warnings when deep reflection mutates a final field (JEP 500)</td><td>26 · final</td><td>Default <code>--illegal-final-field-mutation=warn</code>, one warning per module<br><code>deny</code> is the planned future default</td></tr>\n<tr><td>HTTP/3 in <code>java.net.http.HttpClient</code> (JEP 517)</td><td>26 · final</td><td>Opt-in with <code>HttpClient.Version.HTTP_3</code>; HTTP/2 stays the default<br>Falls back to HTTP/2 or 1.1 unless <code>HTTP_3_URI_ONLY</code> is set</td></tr>\n<tr><td>AOT object caching with any GC (JEP 516)</td><td>26 · final</td><td>The AOT cache from JEP 483 now works with ZGC as well<br>Faster startup no longer rules out a low-pause collector</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hybrid post-quantum key exchange in TLS 1.3 (JEP 527)</td><td>27 · final</td><td>The JDK client offers <code>X25519MLKEM768</code> first by default<br><code>jdk.tls.namedGroups</code> overrides the list</td></tr>\n<tr><td>JFR redacts secrets inside the process (JEP 536)</td><td>27 · final</td><td>Arguments, environment variables and system properties that look secret are written as <code>[REDACTED]</code><br>Tuned with <code>-XX:FlightRecorderOptions</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Structured Concurrency, Lazy Constants, primitive types in patterns</td><td>27 · preview</td><td>Seventh, third and fifth previews<br>Structured Concurrency changed shape again in 26 and 27</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n:::tip Should a service on 25 move?\nUsually not in production: a non-LTS release is superseded within six months, so a fleet on 26 or 27 has to take every feature release after it. Run 27 in CI instead, so the new defaults surface before the next LTS makes them yours.\n:::\n\n**What to try on 27 before it matters:**\n- the full test suite under `--illegal-final-field-mutation=deny`, which finds serializers, mocking and injection code that still writes final fields\n- small containers: a pod with one CPU or under 1792 MB moves from Serial to G1, so compare RSS, pause time and CPU at the same limits\n- memory and tooling with compact headers on: heap size, heap-dump readers, JVMTI agents and anything that reads object layout\n- TLS peers and middleboxes against the larger hybrid key share\n\n**Preview code breaks between releases.** The JDK 25 structured-concurrency example in (01-java-core-jvm.concurrency.q2) does not compile on JDK 27, because `join()` now throws a checked `ExecutionException`.\n\nPin the JDK for every preview example and re-check it on each release (02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q8).\n\n:::deep\n**Which collector did ergonomics pick?** Ask the JVM at the limits it will run under, rather than reading the release notes:\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\"># JDK 21/25 inside a 1-CPU or &lt; 1792 MB container: \"Using Serial\"; JDK 27: \"Using G1\"</span>\njava -Xlog:gc -version\n<span class=\"c\"># the effective flags of a running JVM</span>\njcmd &lt;pid&gt; VM.flags\n<span class=\"c\"># keep the choice explicit instead of inheriting a new default</span>\njava -XX:+UseSerialGC -jar app.jar</code></pre>\n\nSerial was chosen for small machines because it has the least overhead.\n\nJEP 523 expects G1 not to regress footprint, throughput, latency or startup there, but that is a claim to measure on your own pod, not a guarantee — G1's remembered sets and regions cost native memory that a 512 MB limit notices.\n\nCompact headers help most where there are many small objects. The saving is per object, so measure it with the real heap rather than quoting a percentage.\n:::"}]},{"title":"Java vs Go (2025–2026)","items":[{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.java-vs-go-2025-2026.q1","q":"Virtual Threads vs Goroutines — how do the models differ?","a":"Goroutines and virtual threads are runtime-managed concurrency abstractions that can multiplex many activities onto OS threads, but there is no single contract saying they block, cancel, or consume memory in the same way.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Goroutine</th><th>Virtual thread</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Scheduling</td><td>Go runtime scheduler; version, <code>GOMAXPROCS</code>, preemption, and cgroup behavior matter</td><td>JVM scheduler mounts virtual threads on carriers; JDK and executor policy matter</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Blocking integration</td><td>Many standard blocking paths cooperate with the runtime; syscalls and cgo paths need verification</td><td>Supported blocking APIs can park the virtual thread; native calls and library behavior need verification</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cancellation/context</td><td><code>context.Context</code> is an explicit convention; goroutine lifetime is not automatic cancellation</td><td>Interruption and structured scopes can express cancellation; propagation still needs a contract</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Stack and memory</td><td>Stack growth, runtime metadata, heap, and cgo allocations are implementation-dependent</td><td>Continuation, heap objects, carrier state, and <code>ThreadLocal</code> usage are implementation-dependent</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Native boundary</td><td>cgo can change scheduling, pointer/lifetime rules, and thread consumption</td><td>JNI/FFM and monitor behavior can change pinning and failure boundaries by JDK</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Coordination</td><td>Channels, mutexes, atomics, and ownership are chosen for the invariant</td><td>Shared memory, locks, concurrent collections, interruption, and scopes are chosen for the invariant</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Admission</td><td>Bound goroutines with queues, semaphores, connections, and provider quotas</td><td>Bound virtual-thread work with executors, semaphores, connections, and provider quotas</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nChannels and structured scopes solve different problems: one helps coordinate data/ownership, the other helps manage task lifetime. Neither is a direct substitute for the other's cancellation or backpressure semantics.\n\n:::deep\n- Go scheduler behavior is tied to the pinned Go version and runtime settings; do not use a historical stack-size slogan as a capacity promise.\n- A virtual thread may park on a supported blocking operation, but a pinned carrier or a native/library boundary can still consume scarce OS-thread capacity.\n- <code>context.Context</code>, interruption, and structured scopes only work when the called library propagates them. Test cancellation at the database, HTTP, queue, and response boundaries.\n- The count of goroutines or virtual threads is not service capacity. CPU, memory, sockets, database connections, queue depth, and external quotas need explicit bulkheads.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.java-vs-go-2025-2026.q2","q":"How should Java and Go performance be compared for a real backend workload?","a":"A fair Java-versus-Go comparison starts with functional equivalence and a workload card, not a language slogan.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Java variables</th><th>Go variables</th><th>Report</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Runtime</td><td>JDK distribution/version, HotSpot or OpenJ9, GC, JIT, flags</td><td>Go version, GC settings, <code>GOMAXPROCS</code>, cgroup limits</td><td>exact toolchain and image</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Request</td><td>payload, serialization, TLS, fan-out, CPU/blocking mix, cancellation</td><td>the same protocol, client behavior, payload, fan-out, and cancellation</td><td>functional-equivalence checks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Concurrency</td><td>platform/virtual executor, queue/bulkhead, connection pool</td><td>goroutine admission, channel/worker limits, scheduler, connection pool</td><td>in-flight work and downstream saturation</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Memory</td><td>heap/live set, direct/native memory, code cache, thread/carrier state</td><td>heap/live set, goroutine stacks, runtime metadata, cgo/native allocations</td><td>RSS, cgroup memory, and OOM margin</td></tr>\n<tr><td>SLO</td><td>cold readiness, warm p50/p99/p999, throughput, CPU, errors/timeouts</td><td>the same cold/warm and steady-state measures</td><td>distribution, not one favorable run</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Correctness</td><td>ordering, idempotency, deadlines, cancellation, numeric precision, schema</td><td>the same invariants and failure schedule</td><td>contract pass before performance wins</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nKeep framework, database, cache, deployment topology, client, data distribution, quotas, and target latency equivalent. “Startup” means process-ready plus dependency readiness; “memory” means RSS/container usage, not only heap; “throughput” includes errors and tail latency.\n\n:::warn Benchmark validity\nWarm JIT-based services to the state production reaches, avoid coordinated omission in the load generator, use realistic dependencies or faithful fakes, and repeat enough trials to make the interval and variance useful.\n:::\n\nA collector or runtime change can move tail latency without fixing queueing, CPU throttling, network, or dependency limits. Publish the harness, commit, configuration, dataset, hardware, runtime flags, raw results, and date before generalizing the result.\n\n:::deep\nDecide from the measured bottleneck:\n\n- If CPU is material, compare representative hot paths and include warm-up and allocation behavior.\n- If database or partner latency dominates, schema, caching, batching, connection limits, and deadlines may matter more than language microbenchmarks.\n- Price capacity only after fixing region, replicas, autoscaling, SLO, and operational ownership; report cost per successful business operation rather than a universal runtime discount.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.java-vs-go-2025-2026.q3","q":"When do you pick Java, when do you pick Go? What's the polyglot model?","a":"There is no universal language winner. Choose a runtime and deployment target from the service's constraints, then verify the boundary with a contract and capacity test.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Decision signal</th><th>Java candidate</th><th>Go candidate</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Domain complexity</td><td>large stateful domain, mature JVM library, or an existing Java estate</td><td>small focused service where a compact dependency surface is valuable</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Deployment shape</td><td>JVM or Native Image, depending readiness, observability, and dynamic-feature needs</td><td>native-binary deployment when its toolchain and runtime contract fit the service</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Concurrency bottleneck</td><td>virtual threads or bounded executors, with explicit downstream bulkheads</td><td>goroutines or bounded workers, with explicit downstream bulkheads</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Team and operations</td><td>existing JVM skills, libraries, incident tools, and support ownership</td><td>existing Go skills, libraries, incident tools, and support ownership</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Evidence</td><td>matched workload, SLO, RSS/CPU, migration and patching results</td><td>the same workload, SLO, RSS/CPU, migration and patching results</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nA polyglot boundary can be justified when each side has a clear owner and a stable HTTP/gRPC or messaging contract. It is not evidence that one language wins overall: two stacks also mean two build, observability, dependency, hiring, and incident-response surfaces.\n\n:::tip Closing point\nPresent the measured bottleneck, capacity headroom, operational owner, and rollback path. Cost or density numbers are useful only with their workload, date, and deployment assumptions.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- Choose Java for domain and ecosystem constraints when the team can support the JVM or Native Image target.\n- Choose Go for a focused service or native deployment when tests show the binary, scheduler, memory, and operational model meet the SLO.\n- Keep the boundary explicit: protocol, schema, deadlines, retries, idempotency, telemetry, and ownership.\n- Revisit the choice when the workload or support contract changes; do not infer it from company name lists or an unsourced velocity percentage.\n:::","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.java-vs-go-2025-2026.q4","q":"GraalVM Native Image — which Java weakness does it address relative to Go?","a":"GraalVM Native Image is an alternate Java deployment target. It AOT-compiles a reachable application into a native executable, which may improve startup/readiness or density for a particular workload; it is not a guaranteed drop-in equivalent to a HotSpot JVM or a Go binary.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Potential benefit</th><th>Condition to verify</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Startup and readiness</td><td>measure process-ready plus dependency readiness under the same image, probe, and cold-start conditions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Memory and density</td><td>measure RSS/cgroup memory at idle and load; do not infer it from Java heap alone</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Scale-to-zero behavior</td><td>include image pull, initialization, TLS/resource loading, and autoscaling delay in the SLO</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Peak throughput</td><td>compare with a warmed-up JIT using the same protocol, data, concurrency, and error budget</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nThe trade-off is a closed-world build and a separate operational artifact:\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Failure boundary</th><th>Required check</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Reflection, dynamic proxies, and resources</td><td>declare reachability metadata and run end-to-end paths, not only a successful build</td></tr>\n<tr><td>JNI, FFM, and native libraries</td><td>test loading, pointer/lifetime assumptions, platform images, and injected native failures</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Build-time initialization</td><td>check time-sensitive state, configuration, security providers, and side effects moved to build time</td></tr>\n<tr><td>PGO and optimization</td><td>train on representative production paths; treat the profile as build/workload-specific</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Operations</td><td>test metrics, profiles, logs, graceful shutdown, patching, debug symbols, and rollback</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n:::warn Native-image release gate\nA native build is not ready until reachability/metadata tests, representative integration and load tests, TLS/resource loading, startup/readiness, graceful shutdown, observability, and rollback pass for the target image and architecture.\n:::\n\n:::deep\nNative Image can be a good fit when startup or density is an SLO, while a warmed JVM can be a better fit when peak throughput, dynamic framework behavior, or fast build iteration dominates. PGO can help only when its training paths represent production.\n\nTreat Native Image as a separate deployment contract. A passing compilation does not prove reflection, serialization, resource loading, security-provider, JNI/FFM, or failure-path compatibility.\n:::","difficulty":"extra"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.java-vs-go-2025-2026.q5","q":"What's the future of Java: Project Loom, Valhalla, Panama?","a":"The names Loom, Valhalla, and Panama mix delivered features, preview APIs, and active project work. Keep their status separate before making a roadmap claim.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Status anchor</th><th>Safe production statement</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>[[g:Loom]]</td><td>Virtual Threads final in JDK 21; Structured Concurrency preview in JDK 25</td><td>Use the final virtual-thread API; isolate preview structured-concurrency code and flags</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[[g:Valhalla]]</td><td>active project / status can change</td><td>Value/primitive classes are a project direction, not a shipped Java 25 contract</td></tr>\n<tr><td>[[g:Panama]]</td><td>Foreign Function &amp; Memory API final in JDK 22; later work has its own status</td><td>Use the final FFM contract for the selected JDK; do not label every future Panama feature final</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nThe useful direction is clear—better concurrency, data layout, and native interop—without treating the direction as a benchmark or compatibility guarantee.\n\n:::deep\n- Delivered facts belong to a pinned JDK and JEP: Virtual Threads are final in JDK 21 and FFM is final in JDK 22.\n- Preview APIs need matching compiler/runtime flags, an isolated source boundary, and a migration plan.\n- Valhalla remains an active project/unknown from this unit's evidence. Do not promise flat layout, no headers, lower RAM, or cache gains before the target feature and workload exist.\n- Panama is not one status: distinguish FFM final in JDK 22 from later incubator, preview, or project work.\n\nThis wording keeps the Java-versus-Go comparison honest: a delivered runtime contract can be tested; a project goal cannot.\n:::","difficulty":"extra"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.java-vs-go-2025-2026.q6","q":"Records, sealed classes, and pattern matching combine to create what programming style?","a":"Records, sealed classes, and pattern matching support an [[g:ADT-like]] modeling style in Java; they do not create one Java feature formally named “algebraic data types.”\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Construct</th><th>What Java guarantees</th><th>What it does not guarantee</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>record</code></td><td>final component fields and generated data-oriented methods</td><td>deep immutability, validation of every value, or immutable referenced collections</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>sealed</code> hierarchy</td><td>a declared set of permitted direct subclasses known to the compiler</td><td>that persistence, serialization, reflection, or another module will evolve safely</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pattern matching for <code>switch</code></td><td>concise dispatch and exhaustiveness checks for the known type hierarchy</td><td>coverage of <code>null</code>, future variants, or values outside a guard without explicit cases</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nThis style is useful for domain and state-machine modeling with less boilerplate, but “ADT-like” is the accurate label. A Java record is a shallowly immutable carrier, and a sealed hierarchy is nominal and closed only within the rules of the compiled model.\n\n:::deep\nFor a booking or payment state machine, define one record per state and make the switch cover every permitted state, <code>null</code>, and any guarded fallback. Adding a permitted subtype is a source- and deployment-compatibility event, not merely a new data class.\n\nAlso decide the serialization, persistence, default-value, and evolution rules explicitly. A compiler exhaustiveness check protects one compiled boundary; it does not make illegal states impossible across JSON, databases, reflection, or independently deployed services.\n:::","difficulty":"extra"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.java-vs-go-2025-2026.q7","q":"What must a fair Java-versus-Go backend benchmark hold constant?","a":"Hold the question constant before comparing the languages. Use the same protocol, payloads, data distribution, client, TLS settings, serialization, retry/deadline policy, database and cache, connection-pool limits, container CPU/memory quotas, and deployment topology.\n\nMeasure both cold start and warmed steady state; a JIT warm-up, a build-time native image, and a pre-warmed Go process answer different operational questions.\n\nReport throughput and p50/p95/p99 latency together with CPU, RSS/cgroup memory, allocation/GC or runtime pauses, startup/readiness, error/timeout rate, connection-pool wait, queue age, and downstream saturation.\n\nRun multiple trials with a declared ramp, duration, sample size, hardware, compiler/runtime version, and statistical treatment. A lower median with a worse tail or a faster server that overloads the database is not a win.\n\nVirtual-thread or goroutine count is not capacity. Bound CPU work, database connections, sockets, memory, external quota, and in-flight requests in both implementations.\n\nIf Java Native Image is compared with a Go binary, label it as a separate deployment target and test reflection, resource loading, TLS, shutdown, observability, and patching.\n\nThe result should select a workload and operating model, not declare a universal language winner.","difficulty":"advanced"}]}]},"hasVi":true,"stem":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go","sourceOwners":{"10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q1":"design-review-framework","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q2":"design-review-framework","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q7":"design-review-framework","11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q12":"design-review-framework","11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q21":"design-review-framework","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q3":"traffic-caching-building-blocks","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q4":"traffic-caching-building-blocks","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q5":"traffic-caching-buildin